Concurrency = multiple tasks **progressing at the same time**, not necessarily running at the exact same instant.

In Java terms (simple + useful 👇):

---

### 🔹 Basic Example (Single Thread → No Concurrency)
```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        task1();
        task2();
    }

    static void task1() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            System.out.println("Task1: " + i);
        }
    }

    static void task2() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            System.out.println("Task2: " + i);
        }
    }
}
```

➡️ Output: task1 finishes first, then task2 (sequential)

---

### 🔹 Concurrency using Threads
```java
class MyTask extends Thread {
    private String name;

    MyTask(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            System.out.println(name + ": " + i);
        }
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t1 = new MyTask("Task1");
        Thread t2 = new MyTask("Task2");

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}
```

➡️ Output: mixed/interleaved (both tasks progressing together)

---

### 🔹 Better Approach (ExecutorService 🔥)
```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

        Runnable task1 = () -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
                System.out.println("Task1: " + i);
            }
        };

        Runnable task2 = () -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
                System.out.println("Task2: " + i);
            }
        };

        executor.submit(task1);
        executor.submit(task2);

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

