To create a Java program that generates the next 3 execution dates for a given cron expression within a specified date range, you can use the Quartz Scheduler library. You'll need to add the Quartz library to your project.

Here's a Java program to accomplish this task:

```java
import org.quartz.*;
import org.quartz.impl.StdSchedulerFactory;

import java.text.ParseException;
import java.text.SimpleDateFormat;
import java.util.Date;

public class CronNextExecutionDates {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws SchedulerException, ParseException {
        // Input parameters
        String startDateStr = "01-Jan-2023";
        String endDateStr = "31-Dec-2023";
        String cronExpression = "0 0 * * 0 ?";

        // Convert input date strings to Date objects
        SimpleDateFormat dateFormat = new SimpleDateFormat("dd-MMM-yyyy");
        Date startDate = dateFormat.parse(startDateStr);
        Date endDate = dateFormat.parse(endDateStr);

        // Create a SchedulerFactory
        SchedulerFactory schedulerFactory = new StdSchedulerFactory();
        Scheduler scheduler = schedulerFactory.getScheduler();

        // Define a JobDetail with the specified cron expression
        JobDetail jobDetail = JobBuilder.newJob(SimpleJob.class)
                .withIdentity("job1", "group1")
                .build();

        // Define a Trigger with the specified cron expression
        CronTrigger trigger = TriggerBuilder.newTrigger()
                .withIdentity("trigger1", "group1")
                .withSchedule(CronScheduleBuilder.cronSchedule(cronExpression))
                .startAt(startDate)
                .endAt(endDate)
                .build();

        // Schedule the job with the trigger
        scheduler.scheduleJob(jobDetail, trigger);

        // Get the next 3 execution dates
        Date currentTime = new Date();
        int count = 0;

        while (count < 3) {
            Date nextFireTime = trigger.getNextFireTime();
            if (nextFireTime == null || nextFireTime.after(endDate)) {
                break;
            }

            if (nextFireTime.after(currentTime)) {
                count++;
                System.out.println("Next execution date: " + dateFormat.format(nextFireTime));
            }

            trigger = (CronTrigger) trigger.getTriggerBuilder()
                    .startAt(nextFireTime)
                    .endAt(endDate)
                    .build();
        }

        // Shutdown the scheduler
        scheduler.shutdown();
    }

    public static class SimpleJob implements Job {
        @Override
        public void execute(JobExecutionContext context) throws JobExecutionException {
            // This is a simple job, it doesn't do anything here
        }
    }
}
```

Make sure to add the Quartz library to your project's classpath. You can download it from the Quartz Scheduler website or add it as a dependency using a build tool like Maven or Gradle.

